le1\"/\"sample2\" (or \"1\"/\"2\" or \"q1.1\"/\"q1.2\"). Use \"sample1\",\"sample2\" — clear and consistent with the task wording \"sample 2\". fraction rounded to 6 decimal places (keep full precision? 6 digits is enough for a benchmark artifact; but for safety keep more, e.g. 6). I'll write fraction with 6 digits.\n\ndepleted_call.json: {\"depleted_cell_type\": ..., \"cell_type\": ..., ...}. Include several fields for clarity:\n```json\n{\n  \"depleted_cell_type\": \"...\",\n  \"criterion\": \"smallest sample2/sample1 fraction ratio among cell types with sample-1 fraction >= 1%\",\n  \"sample1_fraction\": ...,\n  \"sample2_fraction\": ...,\n  \"ratio_sample2_over_sample1\": ...,\n  \"n_types_considered\": ...,\n  \"all_ratios\": {...}\n}\n```\n\nQC also: report per-sample summary of library sizes and detected genes; also compute doublet-like things? No — keep scope. \n\nOne more QC: verify that all MTX values are non-negative integers (counts). Can quickly assert min data >= 0 and integrality. Add a lightweight check to the script (sample a few elements). Actually checking integrality across all ~10M nnz values is fine with numpy (nnz ~ 9-10M, fast).\n\nRuntime considerations: matrices ~30 MB gzipped MTX; mmread on text MTX may take a while (~1-2 min each). Acceptable for a single run.\n\nMemory: cells x genes sparse float32 ~ 9.2M nnz — no problem.\n\nNow, one small point about \"divide each cell by library size\" — library size = total counts per cell (column sum in original orientation). If a cell has library size 0 (empty), division by zero; check if there are zero-library cells; if any, document and leave as zeros (set inv=0). Let me confirm no zero-library cells — the QC will show the minimum library size.\n\nAlso \"use the number of columns of the matrix as the denominator for fractions\" — number of columns of the original matrix = number of cells. Same as number of rows after transpose. Good.\n\nNow I'll write the script. In the report, also include a com