8. Uncertainty and limitations\n\nNumbers from the summary JSON (already collected above):\n- sample1: 6295 cells; library min 205, p5 830, median 1999, p95 9250, max 45431; genes detected min 181, median 1166, max 8802; mito median 0.0%, p95 0.13%; lib<500: 43; genes<200: 2; mito>20%: 0; no cells with zero library; counts are non-negative integers; nnz 9,246,637.\n- sample2: 5004 cells; library min 204, p5 1837, median 3197, p95 9954, max 48129; genes detected min 174, median 1671.5, max 9052; mito median 0.04%, p95 0.13%; lib<500: 29; genes<200: 5; mito>20%: 0; nnz 9,953,348.\n- Margins: s1 median 0.994, p10 0.146, fraction <0.1: 6.9%; s2 median 2.177, p10 0.277, fraction <0.1: 4.3%.\n- Shared markers RLBP1 (RPE/Müller), SLC1A3 (astrocyte/Müller).\n- Average winning score table (from summary).\n\nEvidence for depletion: horizontal cells are 237/6295 (3.76%) in s1 vs. 49/5004 (0.98%) in s2; ratio 0.260, minimum among the 7 candidate types ≥1% in s1; astrocyte is next at 0.274. Absolute counts also dropped 237→49 despite sample 2 having fewer cells overall (5004 vs. 6295) — the expected count if composition were preserved is ≈ 188 (0.037649*5004), observed 49, roughly 4x depletion. Also, mean horizontal-cell marker expression across all cells dropped 0.088→0.063.\n\nCaveats: astrocyte is close (0.274 vs. 0.260) — however, per the frozen rule, the call is deterministic. Marker-only scoring; no statistical test; no cell filtering; shared markers; 6.9%/4.3% of cells have ambiguous assignments (margin <0.1).\n\nNow, write report.md and delete the temporary scripts. The report is a deliverable — write it carefully but concisely. Also, should analysis_summary.json be kept? This is an intermediate file inside output/; harmless and useful (referenced by the report). Keep it and mention it in the report.\n\nCleanup: delete _verify.py and _verify2.py."}}
